"Proceedings of the International Congress of Mathematicians 2026 - Volume 3: Invited Lectures (Sections 1–4) | Rigidity in Complex Dynamics: Multiplier Spectrum and Dynamical André–Oort Conjecture (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/full/10.1137/25M1805576)\nciteturn6search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Replace the multipliers by its norm in the definition of multiplier spectrum, and one gets the definition of the length spectrum. ... In [38], Ji and the author proposed the following conjecture, which is the analogy of Theorem 5.3 for length spectrum.\n## 4 Multiplier spectrum and length spectrum.\n\nAs \\({\\mathcal M}_d\\) is affine, it is natural to study its ring of functions. There is a natural dynamically meaningful family of morphisms from \\({\\mathcal M}_d({\\mathbb C})\\) to affine spaces, which we call the multiplier spectrum morphisms. They give a natural system of functions on \\({\\mathcal M}_d({\\mathbb C})\\), which can be viewed as analogies of the theta functions on the moduli space of elliptic curves (cf. [57, Remark 5.9]).\n\nFor every \\(f\\in \\textrm {Rat}_d({\\mathbb C})\\) and \\(n\\geq 1\\), denote by \\(\\textrm{Fix}(f^n)\\) the multiset of \\(f^n\\)-fixed points. Then \\(\\textrm{Fix}(f^n)\\) has exactly \\(N_n:=d^n+1\\) fixed points counted with multiplicity. For any \\(f^n\\)-fixed point \\(x\\), the differential \\(df^n(x)\\in {\\mathbb C}\\) is called the multiplier of \\(f^n\\) at \\(x\\). Define\n\n\\begin{align*}S_n(f):=\\{df^n(x)|\\,\\, x\\in \\textrm{Fix}(f^n)\\}\\in {\\mathbb C}^{N_n}/\\Sigma_{N_n},\\end{align*}\n\nwhere \\(\\Sigma_{N_n}\\) is the symmetric group which acts on \\({\\mathbb C}^{N_n}\\) by permuting the coordinates. Via elementary symmetric polynomials, we have \\({\\mathbb C}^{N_n}/\\Sigma_{N_n}\\simeq {\\mathbb C}^{N_n}\\).\n\nDefinition 4.1.\n\nThe multiplier spectrum of \\(f\\) is the sequence \\(S_n(f), n\\geq 1\\).\n\nAccording to the above definition, we need to collect the multipliers of all periodic points. This places the values of the multiplier spectrum in an infinite-dimensional space, which could be inconvenient sometimes. However, by the Noetherianity, we can truncate it to a certain period as follows, obtaining an algebraic morphism \\(\\tau_d\\) with values in a finite-dimensional space while preserving most of the information of the multiplier spectrum. The precise definition is as follows: Since \\(S_n\\) takes the same value in a conjugacy class of rational maps, it descends to a morphism\n\n\\begin{align*}S_n:{\\mathcal M}_d({\\mathbb C})\\to {\\mathbb C}^{N_n}/\\Sigma_{N_n}.\\end{align*}\n\nFor every \\(n\\geq 1\\), define \\(\\tau_{d,n}\\) to be the morphism\n\n\\begin{align*} \\tau_{d,n}:{\\mathcal M}_d({\\mathbb C})&\\to {\\mathbb C}^{N_1}/\\Sigma_{N_1}\\times \\cdots \\times {\\mathbb C}^{N_n}/\\Sigma_{N_n},\\\\ [f]&\\mapsto (S_1(f),\\dots,S_n(f)). \\end{align*}\n\nFor every \\(f\\in \\textrm {Rat}_d({\\mathbb C})\\), set \\(\\tau_{d,n}(f):=\\tau_{d,n}([f])\\). For each \\(n\\geq 1\\), set\n\n\\begin{align*}R_n:=\\{([f],[g])\\in {\\mathcal M}_d({\\mathbb C})^2|\\,\\,\\tau_{d,n}([f])=\\tau_{d,n}([g])\\}.\\end{align*}\n\nThen \\(R_n, n\\geq 1\\) form a decreasing sequence of Zariski closed subsets of \\({\\mathcal M}_d({\\mathbb C})^2\\). By the Noetherianity, the sequence \\(R_n\\) is stable for \\(n\\) sufficiently large. Hence there exists a minimal positive integer \\(m_d\\) such that \\(\\tau_{d,m_d}(f)=\\tau_{d,m_d}(g)\\) implies that \\(\\tau_{d,n}(f)=\\tau_{d,n}(g)\\) for every \\(n\\geq 1\\), i.e., \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) have the same multiplier spectrum. We define\n\n\\begin{align*}\\tau_d:= \\tau_{d,m_d}.\\end{align*}\n\nQuestion 4.2.\n\nWhat is the exact value of \\(m_d?\\)\n\nIt is also interesting to know its lower/upper bounds.\n\nReplace the multipliers by its norm in the definition of multiplier spectrum, and one gets the definition of the length spectrum. More precisely, for every \\(f\\in \\textrm {Rat}_d({\\mathbb C})\\) and \\(n\\geq 1,\\) denote by\n\n\\begin{align*}L_n(f):=\\{|df^n(x)|x\\in \\textrm{Fix}(f^n)\\}\\in {\\mathbb R}^{N_n}/\\Sigma_{N_n}.\\end{align*}\n\nDefinition 4.3.\n\nThe length spectrum of \\(f\\) is defined to be the sequence \\(L_n(f), n\\geq 1\\).\n\nA priori, the length spectrum contains less information than the multiplier spectrum. But in [38, Conjecture 1.10], Ji and the author proposed the following description for rational maps with the same length spectrum.\n\nConjecture 4.4.\n\nLet \\(f,g\\) be non-Lattès rational maps of degree \\(d\\geq 2\\). If \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) has the same length spectrum, then \\(\\tau_d(f)\\) equals to either \\(\\tau_d(g)\\) or \\(\\tau_d(\\overline {g})\\). Here \\(\\overline {g}\\) is the complex conjugation of \\(g\\).\n\nIn 1966, Kac asked the famous question (cf. [39]):\n\n> “Can one hear the shape of a drum?”\n\nKac’s question has been investigated in various settings in dynamics and geometry. In the contexts of geodesic flows on Riemannian manifolds with negative curvature, a long-standing conjecture stated by Burns and Katok [3] (and probably be considered even before) asserted the rigidity of marked length spectrum (for closed geodesics). The surface case was proved by Otal [46] and by Croke [4] independently. A Local version of the Burns and Katok conjecture in any dimension was proved by Guillarmou and Lefeuvre [30]. In one-dimensional real dynamics, it was proved for expanding circle maps (see Shub and Sullivan [56]), and for some unimodal maps (see Martens and de Melo [41] and Li and Shen [40]). It was also studied in dynamical billiards. We refer the readers to Huang, Kaloshin, and Sorrentino [31], Bálint et al. [2], De Simoi, Kaloshin, and Leguil [7], and the references therein.\n\nThe following question can be viewed as Kac’s question in one-dimensional complex dynamics.\n\nQuestion 4.5.\n\nFor \\(f\\in \\textrm {Rat}_d\\), how does \\(S(f)\\) (resp., \\(L(f)\\)) determine \\(f?\\)\n\n#### 5.4.2 Generic injectivity of multiplier spectrum of small periods.\n\nOur definition of \\(\\tau_d\\) requires the use of periodic points of periods not exceeding the number \\(m_d\\), whose precise value is not effectively-known and is probably very large. It is interesting to know whether we can get generic injectivity using only periodic points of small periods. By dimension counting, \\(\\tau_{d,1}\\) is never generically injective except when \\(d=2\\). Again by dimension counting, it is very likely that \\(\\tau_{d,2}\\) is generically injective. For this reason, Ji and the author proposed the following conjecture.\n\nConjecture 5.10 (see [38, Conjecture 1.8]).\n\nFor every \\(d\\geq 2\\), the morphism\n\n\\begin{align*}\\tau_{d,2}:{\\mathcal M}_d({\\mathbb C})\\to {\\mathbb C}^{N_1}/\\Sigma_{N_1}\\times {\\mathbb C}^{N_2}/\\Sigma_{N_2}\\end{align*}\n\nis generically injective.\n\n#### 5.4.3 Generic injectivity of the length spectrum.\n\nIn [38], Ji and the author proposed the following conjecture, which is the analogy of Theorem 5.3 for length spectrum. Note that \\({\\mathcal M}_d\\) is defined over \\(\\mathbb Q\\) (hence over \\(\\mathbb R\\)).\n\nConjecture 5.11 (see [38, Conjecture 1.9]).\n\nFor every \\(d\\geq 2\\), there is a Zariski closed proper subset \\(E_d\\subset {\\mathcal M}_d\\) defined over \\(\\mathbb R\\), such that for every \\([f]\\notin E_d\\), if there is \\(g\\in \\textrm{Rat}_d({\\mathbb C})\\) such that \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) have the same length spectrum, then \\(g\\) or \\(\\overline {g}\\) (the complex conjugation of \\(g\\)) is conjugate to \\(f\\).\n\nBy Theorem 5.3, Conjecture 4.4 implies Conjecture 5.11 directly.\n\n#### 5.4.4 Multiplier spectrum for endomorphisms on \\({\\mathbb P}^{{N}}\\).\n\nFor holomorphic endomorphisms on \\({\\mathbb P}^N\\), \\(N\\geq 2\\), one can also construct the corresponding moduli space \\({\\mathcal M}_d^N({\\mathbb C})\\) [59]. Moreover, the multiplier spectrum morphisms exist on \\({\\mathcal M}_d^N({\\mathbb C})\\) [59]. It is generically finite by Gauthier, Taflin, and Vigny [21, Corollary 2.4]. It is of great interest to extend McMullen’s multiplier spectrum rigidity (Theorem 4.7) and the generic injectivity theorem (Theorem 5.3) to higher dimension.\n\nWe can ask same questions for other families of high-dimensional dynamical systems such as Hénon maps, automorphisms on K3 surfaces.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE MULTIPLIER SPECTRUM MORPHISM IS (https://people.math.harvard.edu/~ctm/home/text/others/xie/multiplier_injective/multiplier_injective.pdf)\nciteturn6search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; ZHUCHAO JI AND JUNYI XIE\nthan the multiplier spectrum. ... parallel result of Theorem 1.3 for length spectrum is true. ... Conjecture 1.6.\n6\nZHUCHAO JI AND JUNYI XIE\nthan the multiplier spectrum. In [JX23b], a parallel result of Theo-\nrem 1.1 has been shown, that is outside the ﬂexible Latt`es family, the\nlength spectrum determines the conjugacy class of rational maps up to\nﬁnitely many choices [JX23b, Theorem 1.5]. We believe the following\nparallel result of Theorem 1.3 for length spectrum is true. Note that\nMd is deﬁned over Q (hence over R).\nWe propose the following conjecture as a parallel result of our The-\norem 1.3.\nConjecture 1.6. For every d ≥2, there is a Zariski closed proper\nsubset Ed ⊂Md deﬁned over R, such that for every [f] /∈Ed, if there\nis g ∈Ratd(C) such that f and g have the same length spectrum, then\ng or g (the complex conjugation of g) is contained in the conjugacy\nclass of f.\nMore precisely, we propose the following description for rational\nmaps with the same length spectrum.\nConjecture 1.7. Let f, g be non-Latt`es rational maps of degree d ≥2.\nIf f and g has the same length spectrum, then τd(f) equals to either\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHomoclinic orbits, multiplier spectrum and rigidity theorems i